// §14 entry 8 -- integer index on `textus` -> EXS-E0311.
//
// §5.1, verbatim (this fixture is that example, wrapped in a function):
//
//   firma t: textus = "café"
//   t.octeti().numerus()      // 5   bytes
//   t.scalares().numerus()    // 4   scalar values
//   t.grapha().numerus()      // 4   grapheme clusters
//   t[0]                      // EXS-E0311: textus has no integer index
//
// `octeti`, `scalares` and `grapha` are distinct types precisely so that a
// byte offset, a scalar-value offset and a grapheme-cluster offset can
// never be silently confused (§2.4: "Byte-index panic / grapheme
// miscount"). Indexing `textus` itself with a bare integer names none of
// the three, so it is rejected rather than guessing which one was meant.
//
// MEASURED 2026-09-25. The fixture's own return type used to be `octetus`,
// which is not a type any section of the spec defines --
// compiler/x86_64/checker/types/prim.inc records that absence as
// deliberate (§5.3's own example writes `*octetus`, but `octetus` bare is
// never declared; §5.1 defines `octeti`, the plural view, not this). That
// drew a spurious EXS-E0301 on the return type ahead of the EXS-E0311 this
// entry is actually about. Changed to `u8` (an eight-bit unsigned integer,
// which is what a byte value is), run directly through `exsc aedifica
// --hospes x86_64-linux --diagnostica json` after b86b080's checker fixes:
// exsc now rejects `t[0]` with exactly {EXS-E0311}, and nothing else.

publica functio primum_octetum(t: textus) -> u8 {
    redde t[0];
}

// TEST: entry=8 shape=code expect-code=EXS-E0311 status=run
